    "q-342424": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342424",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=-4{cos}^{5}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))^{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-4{u}^{5},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-20{u}^{4},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-20{u}^{4}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =20{cos}^{4}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
          "choices": [],
          "dom_index": 0,
          "disabled": false,
