    "q-342446": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342446",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-3(1)(1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =-3x+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
